Infrastructure Explanations
Why each element is added

    3 Firewalls: Added to isolate and secure each server individually, ensuring only legitimate, explicitly permitted traffic can cross the server boundaries.

    1 SSL Certificate: Added to enable cryptographic encryption for the website, transforming connections from insecure http:// to secure https://www.foobar.com.

    3 Monitoring Clients: Added to gather real-time performance metrics and logs from each individual machine and ship them to a central dashboard.

What Firewalls Are For

Firewalls act as security guards for your servers. They inspect incoming and outgoing network traffic based on a defined set of security rules. Their primary goal is to block unauthorized access. For example:

    Server 1 (LB): Allows public traffic only on ports 80 (HTTP) and 443 (HTTPS).

    Servers 2 & 3 (App Nodes): Block all public internet traffic entirely, allowing traffic only if it originates from the Load Balancer’s private IP address.

Why Traffic is Served Over HTTPS

Traffic is served over HTTPS to ensure Confidentiality, Integrity, and Authenticity:

    Encryption (Confidentiality): It encrypts the data passing between the user's browser and the infrastructure, preventing attackers from eavesdropping on sensitive information (like passwords or session tokens).

    Data Integrity: It prevents data from being subtly altered during transit by third parties (man-in-the-middle attacks).

    Authentication: The SSL certificate proves to the browser that the user is actually visiting the real www.foobar.com and not a malicious clone.

What Monitoring is Used For

Monitoring gives visibility into the health and operational status of the infrastructure. It is used for:

    Alerting: Notifying engineers immediately if a server goes down, runs out of memory, or experiences high error rates.

    Troubleshooting: Analyzing historical logs and metric spikes to find the root cause of an outage or slow down.

    Capacity Planning: Tracking long-term resource usage trends (e.g., storage growth) to predict when the infrastructure needs to be scaled up.

How the Monitoring Tool Collects Data

The monitoring clients (such as Sumo Logic Collector, Datadog Agent, or Prometheus Node Exporter) run as background processes (daemons) on each server. They collect data using two main methods:

    System Metrics (Pull/Push): They query local system files (like /proc in Linux) to pull CPU, RAM, network, and disk performance statistics.

    Log Tailing: They constantly read ("tail") local application logs, such as Nginx access logs (/var/log/nginx/access.log) or MySQL error logs, parse them into structured data, and securely stream them over HTTPS to the central monitoring service platform.

How to Monitor Web Server QPS (Queries Per Second)

To monitor QPS, you must capture and count the incoming HTTP requests processed by Nginx within a specific timeframe:

    Ensure Nginx access logging is enabled.

    Configure your monitoring client to parse the Nginx access.log file.

    Every time a new line (request) is added to the log, the client records a timestamped event.

    In your monitoring platform (e.g., Sumo Logic, Grafana), create a dashboard panel using a query that aggregates the total count of log lines divided by time buckets, yielding a real-time Queries Per Second (QPS) graph.

3. Infrastructure Issues & Structural Vulnerabilities

Despite being secured and monitored, this architecture still exhibits architectural bottlenecks:
Issue 1: SSL Termination at the Load Balancer Level

    The Problem: When HAProxy handles "SSL Termination," it decrypts the traffic at Server 1. The data travelling from the Load Balancer to the backend application servers (Servers 2 and 3) is sent as unencrypted, plain HTTP.

    Why it's an issue: If an attacker manages to compromise or sniff traffic within the internal private network layer, they can steal unencrypted user sessions, credentials, and payload data with ease.

Issue 2: Single MySQL Server Accepting Writes

    The Problem: Only the Primary MySQL node (Server 2) can accept write commands.

    Why it's an issue: This represents a major operational bottleneck and a Single Point of Failure (SPOF) for data modification. If Server 2 goes offline, your application becomes completely read-only. Users will not be able to register, log in, or update their data until the database tier is manually reconfigured or fixed.

Issue 3: Servers Running All the Same Components (Mishmash Node Design)

    The Problem: Combining Nginx, the Application runtime, and the MySQL engine together inside the exact same operating system on Servers 2 and 3.

    Why it's an issue: * Resource Contention: MySQL is resource-heavy on RAM and disk I/O, while Nginx/App servers compete heavily for CPU. Under a traffic spike, these services will fight each other for the same system resources, causing the entire server to freeze or crash.

        Scaling Limitations: You cannot scale them independently. If your application logic requires more CPU, you are forced to scale up the database as well, leading to inefficient resource spending.

        Security Risk: If a vulnerability allows a hacker to exploit the web server, they instantly gain local access to the machine where the database data files are stored.
